from register_common import add

add(34,'Grassmannian representability and Plücker construction',[62,63,64,65],2560,2716,'proof sketched','B',
'For a quasi-coherent E on S, the rank-n locally free quotient functor is represented by Grass_n(E), compatible with base change, with its Plücker morphism toP(∧^nE). For finite-rank locally free E, rank r, the standard charts are affine spaces of dimension n(r−n), and Grass_n(E) is smooth and projective/S.',
'Stacks089R proves the finite-free chart construction and base change. The arbitrary quasi-coherent version is obtained by gluing finite quotient charts, but the finite-type/quasi-compact conclusions are not retained without finiteness; that separate assertion is refuted below.',
'Stacks089R,Stacks01OA','Proof: over a chosen invertible n×n minor, a quotient is uniquely represented by a matrix(I_n,A), with n(r−n) free coordinates. Transition functions invert minors and satisfy the cocycle law; the top exterior quotient gives the Plücker coordinates.',group='grassmann')
add(35,'Unconditional quasi-compactness of Grass(E)',[62,63],2560,2640,'false as stated','B',
'The draft asserts quasi-compactness of Grass_n(E) without a finite-generation condition on the quasi-coherent module E, and smoothness for locally free E without retaining finite rank.',
'For E=⊕_(i≥0)k and n=1 the representing projective space is not quasi-compact. The finite-rank theorem in Stacks089R is the valid comparison; usual smoothness also includes local finite presentation.',
'Stacks089R,Stacks01OA','Proof: P(E)=Proj k[x0,x1,…]. The opens D_+(xi) cover it. For any finite subcollection choose j outside its indices; the homogeneous prime generated by all xi except xj is a point missed by that subcollection. Thus no finite subcover exists.',
refine='If E is of finite type, Grass_n(E) is projective and quasi-compact over a quasi-compact base; if E is finite locally free, it is smooth and finitely presented. Without finiteness, retain representability and base change, not these conclusions.',group='grassmann')
add(36,'Grassmannian charts from an exact sequence',[63],2592,2608,'proof sketched','C',
'The chart of rank-n quotients restricting to an isomorphism on E′ is identified canonically with the vector bundle after a basis decomposition of E. The text deletes “sommes directes” in favor of exact sequences but retains the preceding basis choice. Extending “canonical” to an arbitrary exact sequence is a stronger reading, not forced by that context.',
'The chosen-basis chart is valid. For a locally split exact sequence with finite locally free E′ and E″, the chart is the torsor of retractions under Hom(E″,E′); choosing a splitting trivializes it. A nonsplit extension shows that a choice-free global vector-bundle identification is false. Without local splitting even the torsor assertion can fail. Stacks089R uses the valid split coordinate charts. The unresolved issue is the scope of the source’s “une telle suite exacte,” not the standard chart theorem.',
'Stacks089R','Proof: two retractions E→E′ differ by a map E″→E′, and retractions exist locally for a locally split sequence. A global retraction is equivalent to a splitting. A nonzero extension of O by O on a genus1 curve is locally split and has finite locally free terms, but no global retraction because its class in Ext¹(O,O)=H¹(O) is nonzero. Its retraction torsor therefore has no global zero section. This refutes only the unrestricted canonical-trivialization reading. The sequence0→2Z→Z→Z/2→0 has no retraction even after faithfully flat base change, illustrating the necessity of local splitting for the torsor formulation.',
refine='For a locally split exact sequence0→E′→E→E″→0 with E′ and E″ finite locally free, the chart is the retraction torsor under Hom(E″,E′); a chosen splitting identifies it with the corresponding vector bundle. The source’s preceding basis decomposition supplies such a splitting.',group='grassmann')
add(37,'Linear subbundles, duality and rational points',[64,65],2650,2716,'proof sketched','B',
'Projective subbundles correspond to locally free quotients; containment is quotient factorization. For finite rank r, Grass_n(E)≅Grass_(r−n)(E^vee). Rational points of finite-free Grassmannians lift locally in a projective-bundle family, and over infinite fields rational points are dense in each standard affine chart.',
'The quotient chart proof in Stacks089R gives the duality and dimension statements. The asserted intrinsic characterization by degree-one smooth fibers is only proposed for later proof, and is not folded into the proved chart theorem.',
'Stacks089R,Stacks01OA','Proof: dualize0→K→E→Q→0 to obtain E^vee→K^vee. Dualizing again is inverse because all modules are finite locally free. On a chart, lift the finitely many matrix entries. Infinite-field density follows from nonvanishing of polynomials.',group='grassmann')
add(38,'Universal higher-codimension linear incidence',[66,67,68],2720,2819,'proof sketched','B',
'For P(E) of relative dimension r and a universal rank-m quotient of E^vee, the corresponding codimension-m linear subspace is the common zero scheme of m sections ofO(1). Over X→P(E), the incidence has smooth Grassmannian fibers of dimension m(r−m), and all constructions commute with base change.',
'This is the standard Grassmannian construction of Stacks089R applied to the kernel of E_X→O_X(1). The source alternates rank E=r and r+1 and writes an inconsistent Grass index; the stated modern convention fixes those without editing the source.',
'Stacks089R,Stacks01OA','Proof: fixing a point of P^r, codimension-m linear spaces containing it correspond to rank-m quotients of a rank-r vector space, of dimension m(r−m). The global incidence is the associated Grassmannian bundle.',group='higher_sections')
add(39,'Iteration and visibility for higher linear sections',[68,69,70],2820,2888,'proof sketched','B',
'After passage to the complete-flag parameter space, a generic codimension-m section is an iteration of m generic hyperplane sections. It sees a component exactly when its image dimension≥m; geometric irreducibility requires image dimension≥m+1. Regular rank-m sections lower dimension and depth by m.',
'The hyperplane results and the smooth surjective flag-to-Grassmannian map establish these statements. The different generic base fields cannot be identified literally without that base change. SGA7II XI treats complete intersections, while the arbitrary coherent-module exceptional criterion is separately left open in the draft.',
'Stacks089R,Stacks0G4C,SGA7II','Proof: choose a nested flag of quotient subspaces; each successive incidence is a projective bundle over the previous one. Apply the one-hyperplane dimension and primary-field results repeatedly; geometric properties descend along the field extension.',group='higher_sections')
add(40,'Higher-rank exceptional-depth exercise',[70,71],2879,2916,'open on the pages','C',
'The author asks for the correct codimension-m analogues of8.4–8.6 and of the subsequent dual-contact analysis, explicitly allowing Dieudonné to omit the exercise because no essential application is known.',
'Determinantal geometry and iterated Bertini supply much of the construction. Kmentt–Shute2022 Th1.2 proves for geometrically irreducible embedded X⊂P^r and codimension-m linear sections that the bad locus in the Grassmannian has dimension≤dim Grass−dim X+m, over any field; Remark1.5 does not claim the arbitrary-morphism extension as proved. The exact all-m coherent-sheaf/depth exceptional iff in this draft was not matched in EGA/SGA, Jouanolou-related or later searches. That is the residual, not absence of higher-codimension Bertini theory.',
'Stacks089R,Benoist2011,SGA7II,KmenttShute2022',confidence='medium',group='higher_sections')
add(41,'Schubert strata and their nonempty range',[71,72,73],2918,3004,'false as stated','B',
'For n=dim X≥m, the stated relative codimension of the corank≥k singular-incidence stratum is (k−1)(n−m)+k²; the text gives an excessive nonempty range k≤r−m.',
'The dimension formula follows from the standard matrix rank-stratum codimension k(n−m+k), but the nonempty range is k≤min(m,r−n). X=P³ with m=1 has no singular hyperplane stratum, contrary to the printed range. The determinantal statement is established; its malformed range is not.',
'Stacks089R,SGA7II','computations.py schubert_checked_cases and universal_dimension_identities verify200 permitted integer cases and the symbolic identity k(n−m+k)−(n−m)=(k−1)(n−m)+k². proof_checks.md gives the independent rank count and the P³ boundary counterexample.',
refine='In the stated smooth incidence setting, for a nonempty corank-k stratum require1≤k≤min(m,r−n). Its codimension relative to the Grassmannian dimension is (k−1)(n−m)+k²; rank-one-drop is smooth and deeper rank loci have the usual determinantal singularities.',group='schubert')
add(42,'The higher-projection discriminant need not be irreducible',[73,74],3005,3054,'false as stated','B',
'The proposed extension says that the discriminant in the projective parameter space of linear spaces through a generic center is geometrically irreducible of codimension1, including m=1.',
'For a conic projected toP¹ the discriminant consists of two distinct geometric points in characteristic0, so it is not geometrically irreducible. SGA7II Lefschetz pencils assert finitely many ordinary singular values, not that this finite set is irreducible.',
'SGA7II','computations.py conic_pencil_branch_values: on X=P¹ embedded byO(2), the pencil[s²+t²:st] has branch values±2. X is smooth, proper, geometrically irreducible and is itself a degree2 Veronese. The two distinct values persist for a general degree2 pencil.',
refine='For a Lefschetz pencil the bad-value locus is finite and each bad fiber has one ordinary singularity; it need not be geometrically irreducible. For higher-dimensional parameter spaces retain only separately proved codimension bounds.',group='schubert')
add(43,'Artin elementary fibrations',[75,76,77,78,79],3058,3207,'proof sketched','B',
'An elementary fibration is an open in a smooth projective family of geometrically connected curves with nonempty finite étale boundary. Over an infinite perfect field, a smooth point on a geometrically irreducible finite-type scheme has a basis of opens admitting towers of elementary fibrations.',
'The text credits M.Artin. Original SGA4III XI3.1 and3.3, printed68–72, gives the definition and the algebraically closed-field neighborhood theorem at a rational point. Schmidt–Stix2016 Lemma6.3 explicitly proves the infinite-perfect-field extension, including nonrational points, by an open Grassmannian condition and finite Galois conjugates. The source proof has local-choice gaps, so these later exact results are not a certificate of each sentence here.',
'SGA4XI,SchmidtStix2016,PSVAppendix2009','Targeted proof check: Artin XI3.3 was read with its algebraically closed-field hypothesis. Schmidt–Stix Lemma6.3 and its full proof were read: the good choices over a geometric point form a nonempty open in the Grassmannian; intersect its finitely many Galois conjugates and use rationality of the Grassmannian over the infinite field. Induction gives the tower. Attribution of the original construction remains Artin.',group='artin')
add(44,'Higher-degree and finite-set Artin neighborhood variants',[79,80,81],3208,3300,'open on the pages','C',
'The notes ask for higher-degree fixed centers, finite affine sets of closed points, non-geometrically-irreducible versions with relaxed fiber connectedness, and removal of the infinite-field hypothesis.',
'The finite-set infinite-field construction appears in Panin–Stavrova–Vavilov2009 Appendix, Proposition1 with proof; its perfect-field case suffices here. The finite-field, smooth geometrically irreducible affine finite-set version is Panin2025 Th5.3, with an earlier statement/sketch in Panin2019 §2 and AppendixB. These establish the specified smooth affine field cases. They do not automatically prove the separate arbitrary relative-base variant or an unspecified relaxation of geometric connectedness.',
'SGA4XI,Panin2025,Panin2019,PSVAppendix2009','The opened2009 Appendix pp1–4 gives fixed-point high-degree Bertini and the resulting elementary fibration. The opened2025 theorem includes an additional closed codimension-one Z whose intersection maps finitely and surjectively to the base. Its proof uses finite-field Bertini; no claim that degree2 alone suffices over finite fields is made.',group='artin_finite')
add(45,'Relative elementary-fibration proposal',[81,82,83],3270,3386,'open on the pages','C',
'The proposed relative version starts with a projective flat family with geometrically irreducible R2 fibers, a finite subscheme of smooth points and a prescribed closed subset to avoid; it asks for a relative elementary neighborhood after suitable linear sections.',
'Artin’s field theorem and Panin2025 settle important field cases; Guo–Panin’s weak elementary fibrations over more general bases are a related but different conclusion. The full relative statement here lacks precise residue-field and base-change assumptions and was not matched exactly. Those specifications are the residual task.',
'SGA4XI,Panin2025,GuoPanin2023',confidence='medium',group='artin_relative')
add(46,'Complex poly-elementary neighborhoods are K(pi,1)',[83,84],3340,3427,'proof sketched','B',
'For a complex poly-elementary neighborhood of dimension n, the analytic space is a K(pi,1); its fundamental group is built by successive extensions by finitely generated free groups, and its universal cover is homeomorphic toC^n. This is a topological, not biholomorphic, assertion.',
'SGA4III XI’s comparison argument uses elementary neighborhoods and their topological fibration structure. A punctured compact complex curve has contractible universal cover; the tower gives asphericity. The stronger homeomorphism statement uses triviality of a smooth bundle over a contractible base, not uniformization in several variables.',
'SGA4XI','Proof: compactify each curve fibration with finite étale boundary. Ehresmann’s theorem for the compact pair gives a locally trivial bundle of punctured surfaces. Pull to the universal cover of the base; the bundle over its contractible R^(2n−2) base is trivial, and then take the contractible surface cover R². The homotopy exact sequence gives the free-group extension and vanishing higher homotopy.',group='artin')
add(47,'Generic linear projection and its incidence resolution',[85,86,87],3432,3554,'proof sketched','B',
'A center C⊂P^r of codimension m+1 gives P^r−C→P^m. For X→P^r and a regular inverse-image axis, the incidence resolution is the blow-up of that axis. If dim f(X)≤m, a generic center misses X.',
'This is the standard linear-projection and Rees construction, compatible with Stacks01OF. The following uniform-fiber assertion is a separate false claim; the blow-up description itself does not give it.',
'Stacks01OF,Stacks089R','Proof: write the projection using m+1 linear forms. Their common zero ideal is the axis; the closure of the graph is Proj of the Rees algebra. For a regular sequence the expected incidence equations present this algebra. Dimension of the incidence of centers meeting a set of dimension≤m is strictly less than the full center Grassmannian.',group='projection')
add(48,'Generic fiber dimension does not control all projected fibers',[87,88],3516,3554,'false as stated','B',
'Proposition14.1a uses only the generic fiber dimensions of components of f:X→P^r to conclude the expected dimension for every fiber of a generic linear projection.',
'Jumping fibers of f remain after generic projection. The same blow-up example underlying Poonen–Slavov Example1.7 refutes the assertion and the unrestricted flatness consequence. Constant-dimensional nonempty f-fibers or a quasi-finite f are genuinely stronger hypotheses.',
'PoonenSlavov2022','Proof: let f:Bl_pP³→P³ and m=2. A generic center C is a point distinct from p. In the incidence resolution for projection from C, the exceptionalP² over p survives and maps to one point ofP², giving a2-dimensional fiber instead of the asserted1. All components of f have generic fiber dimension0; X is smooth and proper.',
refine='For the stated projection-dimension conclusion, impose the constant-dimension hypotheses on all nonempty f-fibers as in14.1b, or restrict to quasi-finite f and the standard generic linear-projection setting. Generic fiber dimension alone is insufficient.',group='projection')
add(49,'Noether normalization by projection',[88],3555,3561,'proof sketched','E',
'For an m-dimensional projective X with a finite map toP^r, a generic linear projection avoiding the image gives a finite surjection X→P^m; the quasi-finite version applies on the corresponding open and is dominant.',
'This is the classical projective Noether-normalization argument. The finite-image and dimension hypotheses separate it from the false arbitrary-morphism14.1a. Standard linear incidence and the proper quasi-finite⇒finite theorem give the exact conclusion.',
'Stacks089R,EGAIV3','Proof: a generic center of codimension m+1 misses the m-dimensional image. The projection has zero-dimensional fibers by the standard projective dimension argument. Proper plus quasi-finite is finite; its image is closed of dimensionm inP^m, hence all ofP^m.',group='projection')
add(50,'Cohen–Macaulay flatness needs equidimensional fibers',[88],3562,3584,'false as stated','B',
'Corollary14.3 infers flatness of the projected Cohen–Macaulay scheme from14.1a without excluding jumping f-fibers.',
'Miracle flatness applies once the required dimension equality over the regular target is known. The smooth blow-up counterexample to14.1a also refutes this unrestricted corollary. The later explicitly quasi-finite version has the needed dimensions.',
'EGAIV3,Stacks00R4,PoonenSlavov2022','Proof: in the example ofC-SA23-hyp-048, X and the blow-up at the disjoint projection axis are smooth, hence CM. The fiber dimension jumps from1 to2, so the morphism to smoothP² is not flat. Thus CM of the source alone does not repair14.1a. The full statement and induction proof of Stacks10.128.1 (00R4) were read: regular noetherian local base, CM noetherian local source and the dimension equality are all required.',
refine='A finite-type morphism from a Cohen–Macaulay scheme to a regular scheme is flat at a point when the local dimension equality of miracle flatness holds. Apply this only after proving the all-fiber dimension statement, for example in the draft’s quasi-finite projection setting.',group='projection')
add(51,'Prescribed finite points under projection',[89],3588,3622,'false as stated','B',
'The source asks a generic projection to avoid a closed subset Y while preserving a prescribed finite set T, but does not state Y∩T=∅. It further asks unramifiedness at all points over the image of T for an unramified, not necessarily immersive, f.',
'The avoidance assertion needs disjointness. Unramifiedness at the chosen smooth points is the usual tangent-space avoidance result; unramifiedness at every other point of the same projected fiber is stronger and fails for the allowed disconnected immersion components.',
'EGAIV4,Stacks0FD4','Proof: Y=T={t} already contradicts simultaneous avoidance/preservation. For the stronger fiber assertion let X be the disjoint union of a smooth plane curve and a nodal plane curve, both mapped by closed immersions toP², with t on the smooth curve at the image of the other node. Projection toP¹ cannot be unramified at that node: its embedding dimension2 exceeds that of the target1.',
refine='Require Y∩T=∅ for avoidance, and assert unramifiedness at the prescribed smooth points ofT. Any conclusion for all points over their images requires additional hypotheses excluding singular or colliding branches.',group='projection')
add(52,'Birational projection versus étale at singular points',[90],3623,3629,'false as stated','B',
'For geometrically reduced immersed X of dimension≤m−1, the birational-projection statement is supplemented by étaleness at every point over a prescribed finite set, without requiring those points to be smooth.',
'Generic birational projection is standard. The added étaleness claim fails at singular points whose embedding dimension drops in projection. This does not refute the birational part.',
'EGAIV4,Stacks089R','Proof: take the union of three noncoplanar projective lines inP³ through p, over an algebraically closed field. It is reduced of dimension1≤2−1. A general projection toP² gives three concurrent plane lines, birational on every component. At p the source embedding dimension is3 and target image embedding dimension2, so the induced map is not étale.',
refine='A generic projection of a geometrically reduced immersed X toP^m with dim X≤m−1 is birational onto its image. Étaleness at selected points is asserted only under appropriate smoothness and tangent-separation hypotheses.',group='projection')
add(53,'Birational hypersurface models',[90,91],3630,3687,'proof sketched','E',
'An irreducible geometrically reduced projective n-fold over an infinite field admits a birational morphism onto a hypersurface inP^(n+1). This is not generally an embedding. The projected ramification locus has no prescribed smooth point forced into every generic choice; this does not make it globally empty.',
'This is classical generic projection/Noether normalization. The distinction between birational morphism and closed immersion is essential already for smooth curves of genus2. The draft’s connected proper étale-cover comparison withP^m is valid over an algebraically closed field.',
'EGAIV4,Stacks089R','computations.py genus_two_no_plane_degree checks small degrees; the full argument is that(d−1)(d−2)/2 jumps from1 atd=3 to3 atd=4 and increases thereafter, so no smooth plane curve has genus2. Therefore a smooth genus2 curve cannot satisfy a stronger plane-embedding reading.',group='projection')
add(54,'Axiomatic local Bertini',[92,93,94,95],3690,3837,'proof sketched','C',
'The proposed fp incidence framework assumes a flat geometrically irreducible parameter G, smooth geometrically irreducible incidence fibers over P, a primitive visibility axiom, and a tangent-incidence dimension bound. These yield the stated visibility, dimension, local-property and generic-smoothness conclusions.',
'The incidence dimension argument is sound with those axioms. Later axiomatic Bertini frameworks (Cumino–Greco–Manaresi, Spreafico) are relevant, but searches did not identify an exact theorem with this entire package. They are not substituted for the unproved global irreducibility implication.',
'Stacks0FD4,Spreafico1998,CuminoGrecoManaresi1986','Proof: if the singular choices above an n-dimensional tangent space have dimension≤N−n−1, their incidence over n-dimensional X has dimension≤N−1 and cannot dominate N-dimensional G. This proves generic smoothness under the explicitly stated quasi-finite/tangent hypotheses only.',confidence='medium',group='axiomatic')
add(55,'Axiomatic global irreducibility and connectedness',[94,95],3790,3854,'open on the pages','C',
'The author asks Serre or Zariski whether his axioms imply a generalized Bertini–Zariski irreducibility theorem; he explicitly suspends sections3 and6 pending that answer and recommends extending exceptional-locus results.',
'The classical and modern Bertini theorems cited above establish substantial special cases. No exact implication from the draft’s full incidence axioms was identified in the targeted searches. The residual is the global irreducibility axiom/theorem; local transversality dimension bounds alone have not been treated as a proof.',
'Stacks0G4C,Spreafico1998,CuminoGrecoManaresi1986',confidence='medium',group='axiomatic')
